 y^2\\).\n\n1. If \\(x = (2 + \\sqrt{3})y\\), then\n\n\\[x^2 + y^2 = [(2 + \\sqrt{3})y]^2 + y^2 = (4 + 4\\sqrt{3} + 3)y^2 + y^2 = (7 + 4\\sqrt{3})y^2.\\]\n\n2. If \\(x = (2 - \\sqrt{3})y\\), then\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"e837686d0e0ab40129fe92d27239320fe774eb1fe3da97d8deb3514632475109"},"seconds":20.098541736602783,"seed":193259790421622064,"stop":"eos","text":"\\[x^2 + y^2 = [(2 - \\sqrt{3})y]^2 + y^2 = (4 - 4\\sqrt{3} + 3)y^2 + y^2 = (7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2.\\]\n\nTo find the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\), we compare the two expressions:\n\n\\[(7 + 4\\sqrt{3})y^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2.\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732\\), it follows that \\(7 - 4\\sqrt{3} < 7 + 4\\sqrt{3}\\). Therefore, the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\) occurs when \\(x = (2 - \\sqrt{3})y\\), and the minimum value is:\n\n\\[(7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2.\\]\n\nTo find the minimum value, we need to check if \\(y\\) can be zero. If \\(y = 0\\), then from the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 4xy\\), we also get \\(x = 0\\). Hence, \\(x^2 + y^2 = 0\\). Therefore, the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\) is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{0}.\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{0}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"\\[x^2 + y^2 = [(2 - \\sqrt{3})y]^2 + y^2 = (4 - 4\\sqrt{3} + 3)y^2 + y^2 = (7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2.\\]\n\nTo find the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\), we compare the two expressions:\n\n\\[(7 + 4\\sqrt{3})y^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2.\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732\\), it follows that \\(7 - 4\\sqrt{3} < 7 + 4\\sqrt{3}\\). Therefore, the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\) occurs when \\(x = (2 - \\sqrt{3})y\\), and the minimum value is:\n\n\\[(7 - 4\\sqrt{3})y^2.\\]\n\nTo find the minimum value, we need to check if \\(y\\) can be zero. If \\(y = 0\\), then from the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 4xy\\), we also get \\(x = 0\\). Hence, \\(x^2 + y^2 = 0\\). Therefore, the minimum value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\) is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{0}.\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:580"}
